From: Qianhui Xu <xiaqinghuan@mail.ustc.edu.cn>
Subject: [PATCH 0/1] power: supply: qcom_battmgr: allow writing the USB
 input current limit

A USB 2.0 host port (SDP) may supply 500 mA once the device behind it is
configured, but the Battery Management (battmgr) firmware does not classify
such a port: it reports usb_type "Unknown" and keeps the USB input current
limit at 100 mA, the value that applies before configuration.  On the
Lenovo Legion Y700 Gen4 (SM8750, TB322FC) the USB link is high speed and
configured, yet with a data cable to a PC the device runs a net discharge
of about 0.8 A while the battery status still reads "Charging"; that status
field mirrors the firmware's charge-enable state, not the direction of the
current.

Nothing in mainline can change that limit.  qcom-battmgr-usb exports
POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_INPUT_CURRENT_LIMIT read-only, and the vendor charger
driver rides a PMIC glink owner that does not exist upstream, so the only
way to raise the limit today is firmware policy.  The write half of the
protocol the driver already reads does exist in the driver, though:
BATTMGR_USB_PROPERTY_SET (0x33) and USB_INPUT_CURR_LIMIT (5) are defined in
qcom_battmgr.c and were never referenced, the property request helper and
the request/response payloads are in place, and the battery power supply
already has a writable property through its own set_property callback.

Patch 1/1 wires that up for the sm8350 variant.  set_property for
POWER_SUPPLY_PROP_INPUT_CURRENT_LIMIT sends opcode 0x33 with property 5;
the requested value is kept inside [500 mA, max(CURRENT_MAX, 900 mA)]
because the firmware rejects values outside its range; firmware errors are
returned to the caller instead of being dropped; and the ack for opcode
0x33 is handled in both variant callbacks, so a successful write is no
longer logged as "unknown message 0x33".  sc8280xp and x1e80100 are left
untouched and return -EINVAL.

Measured on the device with one kernel built from this series and booted
with "fastboot boot" (nothing written to storage), data cable to a USB 2.0
host port.  First column is the value written to input_current_limit and
the readback; the third column is qcom-battmgr-bat's current_now, sampled
five times per step, 4 s apart:

  write     readback   current_now (mA)
  500000    500000     -155,    0, -115, -166, -160
  900000    900000     -161, -274, -482, -488, -495
  500000    500000     -492, -204, -102, -139, -140
  900000    900000     -152, -362, -505, -523, -527

"unknown message" lines in dmesg over the whole run: 0.  Requests outside
the range are clamped by the driver rather than rejected silently: writing
1500000 reads back 900000, writing 250000 reads back 500000.  Each step
carries the tail of the previous setting in its first samples, so the last
samples of a step are the ones to compare.

Three boundaries on what this proves:

  1. BATTMGR_USB_PROPERTY_SET is not a documented interface.  We know the
     firmware accepts opcode 0x33 with property 5 and acts on it because
     the reply stops being reported as an unknown message and because the
     reported battery current follows the requested limit repeatably across
     two rounds.  There is no firmware documentation behind that opcode and
     property number; if someone knows the real contract, this driver would
     rather follow it.

  2. The measurements come from a throwaway test kernel booted with
     "fastboot boot", not from the kernel this device runs day to day.  The
     patch is not part of any shipped payload yet.

  3. The 900 mA steps cannot be shown to be honoured end to end: a USB 2.0
     SDP port may supply at most 500 mA, so this host caps the current near
     500 mA whichever value the firmware was asked for.  The series claims
     that the limit is accepted and applied, not that the whole advertised
     range is reachable on an SDP port.

Not tested, and therefore not claimed:

  - sc8280xp and x1e80100 variants: compile only.  The new callback returns
    -EINVAL for them on purpose, so their behaviour cannot change.
  - The limit was not re-read after an unplug/re-plug.  Nothing in the
    driver re-applies it, so if the firmware resets the limit on a new
    charger notification, userspace has to write it again; which of the two
    happens was not measured.
  - Charge time and port-side current were not measured; only the values
    the battery power supply reports.

Open questions:

  - Is the 500 mA floor the right place for kernel policy, or should the
    driver clamp only to the firmware's CURRENT_MAX and leave a lower limit
    to userspace?
  - If the limit is firmware state that a charger notification can reset,
    should the driver re-apply the last written value on
    NOTIF_USB_PROPERTY (0x32), or is that userspace's job?

